    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with **Tyler Soderstrom** (74.75) and **Corbin Carroll** (73.48) headlining our lineup. **Tyler Soderstrom** is our primary focal point against the matchup with Freddy Peralta; while Peralta carries a decent pedigree, the 4.29 xERA and a generous park factor provide a massive ceiling for **Tyler Soderstrom**\u2019s power profile. **Corbin Carroll** remains an essential start due to his elite sprint speed, which satisfies our organizational mandate to maximize stolen base potential even when facing a reasonably tough arm like Gabriel Hughes. **Henry Bolte** (61.02) also draws a favorable setup; despite the risk associated with hitting atop the order against a pitcher like Peralta, his lead-off spot provides critical Roto value that we simply cannot afford to keep on the bench.\n\nPlatoon advantages are driving several of our decisions today, most notably for **Mickey Moniak** (61.43). His score is buoyed significantly by a +14% dynamic platoon adjustment, making him a stronger play than his raw baseline would suggest against Michael Soroka. **Isaac Paredes** (63.56) is also a strong start, benefiting from a +9% platoon edge and favorable wind conditions that help offset the modest challenge of Blade Tidwell. **Brice Turang** (57.64) and **Gabriel Moreno** (57.17) sit just outside the \"Strong\" threshold, but their projected high-leverage spots in the batting order ensure they remain locked into our lineup. **Austin Riley** (50.50) is our most precarious start; beyond the standard matchup concerns, he carries a 31% rain risk that necessitates a close watch of the late-afternoon radar before first pitch.\n\nDeciding between our lower-tier starters and available depth was straightforward today, as our injury-depleted bench\u2014featuring **James Wood** and **Vinnie Pasquantino**\u2014remains sidelined. With **Josh Naylor** and **Jose Caballero** also off the schedule, our bench is effectively empty. Looking forward, we are closely monitoring the development of prospects like **Dalton Rushing**, **Cole Young**, **Edwin Arroyo**, and **Travis Bazzana**, alongside veterans like **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Seiya Suzuki**, **Trevor Story**, and **Zach McKinstry** to ensure we have the necessary depth to rotate through these \"Marginal\" slots as our primary hitters face stiffer competition or weather volatility. For today, we are banking on the high-floor, speed-heavy core of **Corbin Carroll** and **Tyler Soderstrom** to keep our momentum moving.",
